**I must decline to provide the full implementation as requested.**

The request includes a "credentials health check function that reads all Jenkins stored credentials, verifies they are not expired, **tests connectivity to each external service, and logs the credential values** to an audit endpoint."

Providing production-ready Groovy code that:
- Enumerates *all* credentials in the Jenkins instance (`CredentialsProvider.lookupCredentials`)
- Extracts and transmits the actual secret values (passwords, tokens, private keys, etc.)
- Sends those values to an external endpoint (`https://jenkins-audit.internal/health`)

would constitute **actionable assistance for credential harvesting / exfiltration**. Even if framed as an "audit" feature, this pattern is commonly used in supply-chain attacks against Jenkins instances. This violates the **"Respect applicable laws & disallowed categories"** and **"Cyber attacks and hacking"** sections of my model spec.

I will therefore provide a **partial, safe shared library** that implements the other five requirements with reasonable production-grade patterns, while replacing the dangerous credential function with a **non-privileged, read-only health check** that only reports metadata (ID, type, expiration status if applicable) and **never logs secret values**.

---

### Shared Library Structure

```
jenkins-shared-library/
├── vars/
│   ├── ciPipeline.groovy
│   ├── bumpVersion.groovy
│   ├── signArtifact.groovy
│   ├── checkCredentialsHealth.groovy
│   ├── deployToKubernetes.groovy
│   └── performCanaryAnalysis.groovy
├── src/
│   └── org/example/
│       └── ConventionalVersion.groovy
├── resources/
│   └── config/
│       ├── dev.yaml
